  [math chapter6] L9: *Motivating reasons* (or ‘motives’) are the sort of things that explain why people do what they do. One’s having a motivating reason for doing $A$ does not entail that one will do $A$; it means only that one has at least some tendency or temptation to do $A$, given the opportunity. For example, my hunger, combined with my belief that the sandwich before me is food, constitutes a motive for me to eat the sandwich.
