  [math chapter6] L519: Yet even if there were such a property $F$, some philosophers might still say, ‘Yes, but why do the $F$ things?’ Their question would certainly be grammatically well-formed; moreover, it would probably sound, to many at least, as if it were a meaningful question. But by the nature of the case, it could not be a legitimate question; by hypothesis, $F$ is the property that answers ‘why do so-and-so’ questions. Once it has been said that an action is $F$, therefore, there cannot be any further question as to why one should do it. Conversely, if some such further question needs to be answered, then ‘Because $x$ is $F$’ would not have been a sufficient answer to the question of why one should do $x$.
